+---
+
+## 43. The images arrived
+
+### F-115 · All 28 slots filled, every dimension correct on the first pass · P2
+
+Two uploads, byte-identical (`cmp` confirms the `(1)` was a duplicate). 28 files,
+every filename matching its manifest id exactly, and every intrinsic size
+matching what the manifest declares — 23 at 1600×900, five `og-*` at 1200×630.
+Nothing had to be renamed, rescaled or re-cropped.
+
+That is what the manifest bought. The brief was generated from the same file the
+component renders from (`pnpm images:brief`), so the names and sizes an agent was
+given could not drift from the ones the code expects.
+
+Spot-checked against their prompts: the three substrate assemblies read
+correctly (fasteners into plywood, ridged adhesive over slab, tubes under a
+floated assembly), cupping curves the right way with moisture indicated from
+below, and **no text appears in any image checked** — the prohibition held.
+
+### F-116 · 12.1 MB → 1.0 MB, with no visible difference · **P1**
+
+The delivered files averaged 435 KB each; one was 782 KB. For flat vector art on
+a plain background that is roughly an order of magnitude more than it needs — the
+encoder had been asked for near-lossless output on images consisting mostly of
+one flat colour.
+
+Re-encoded at `cwebp -q 88 -m 6`: **12.1 MB → 1,051 KB, a 92% reduction.**
+Measured difference against the originals: mean 1.05/255 per channel (0.4%), max
+21 on hard edges. Rendered and compared side by side before committing to it.
+
+`next/image` re-encodes on delivery, so this was never about what a visitor
+downloads. It is about the 12 MB every clone of this repository would have
+carried permanently, in every deploy payload, forever.
+
+### F-117 · The guard's exists-on-disk check had never actually run · **P1, my error**
+
+`verify-images.mjs` shipped with this:
+
+```js
+const DEFAULT_PENDING = /status: 'pending'/.test(src);
+```
+
+Every helper-built entry hardcoded `status: 'pending'`, so that regex always
+matched, so **every entry was treated as pending and the "published file must
+exist" check never executed on anything.** It reported `28 pending` and looked
+correct, because at the time all 28 genuinely were.
+
+The moment they became published it would have silently passed a manifest
+pointing at files that were not there. A guard that is structurally incapable of
+failing is worse than no guard, because it is counted as coverage.
+
+Now `DEFAULT_STATUS` is a single named constant in the manifest, the guard reads
+it, and hand-written literals carry their own status. Deleting one file
+reproduces the failure it is supposed to catch:
+
+```
+✗ "og-data" is marked published but public/illustrations/og-data.webp does not exist
+```
+
+The manifest also now documents that a **new** slot must be written as a full
+object literal with `status: 'pending'` — deliberately more work than calling the
+helper, so the awkward path is the one that ships a placeholder to production.
+
+### F-118 · Five social cards existed and nothing pointed at them · P2
+
+`og-framework`, `og-market`, `og-glossary`, `og-standards` and `og-data` were
+generated, verified and sitting unused. Same defect family as F-73: an asset that
+exists, is correct, and has no path to it.
+
+All five are now the `openGraph.images` entry for their page, with explicit
+`width` and `height` so a card renderer does not have to fetch the file to lay
+it out.
+
+### The zips came out of the repository
+
+Both uploads were tracked at the root: 12.7 MB each, 25.4 MB together, for
+content now stored decoded under `public/illustrations/` at 1 MB. `git rm
+--cached` on both. Git keeps them in history regardless — the objects are
+written and cannot be un-written without a rewrite nobody should do for 25 MB —
+but no future clone checks them out into a working tree.
